xact isolation mechanism the slice depends on slow, disk-heavy, or unsafe if two Editor instances contend for one Library. If a scoped-but-stale-data write executes inside a worktree with unresolved Library-cache behavior, the most likely failure mode is a corrupted or silently wrong Unity scene/asset commit -- precisely the class of failure the entire evidence/conformance architecture exists to prevent -- occurring on the very first real exercise of write capability, in a slice sized more like a multi-week initiative than \"the minimum next increment.\"","hidden_assumptions":["That 'one small write-capable ticket' remains small once it actually includes write capability + worktree isolation + a diff-scope check + a ported GER loop + a path-existence validator + task re-verification -- treated as a single slice rather than several.","That grep-confirmed fixes to 13-16 stale paths are sufficient, rather than a full manual audit of all 37 contracts, before trusting exclusive_resources as a write-scoping boundary.","That NSC-037 and NSC-024 are actually free of the same graph/prose-mismatch defect class just discovered in NSC-003 -- asserted as a 'reviewer-identified candidate' without verification.","That Git worktrees are a solved, negligible-cost mechanism for Unity execution, despite no planning document anywhere addressing Library/ cache reimport time, disk cost, or Editor-instance contention.","That a 'lightweight section-anchor' GDD-drift check is cheap to build, when the current schema has no section-addressable structure to anchor against -- only literal copied text.","That fail-closed dispatch fully neutralizes the operational severity of the stale-path and NSC-003 defects, even though the synthesis's own critical_risks language treats them as urgent/must-fix-before-anything.","That the best task to build the pipeline's proof-of-concept on is the easiest one to push through cleanly, rather than the one the human developer actually wants built next."],"ignored_minority_arguments":["unity_production_engineer's concrete, unaddressed Unity Library/worktree-cache cost problem never appears in the synthesis's preserve/change_or_replace/defer_or_delete/critical_risks/next_slice sections, despite the synthesis explicitly recommending worktree-based write execution as the next step.","unity_production_engineer's GUID-vs-path-string asset-identity flaw (the root cause behind the stale-path defect, likely to recur on any future rename) is downgraded by the synthesis to a simple existence-check fix rather than an identity-model fix.","The manual/interactive camp's (unity_production_engineer, adversarial_qa, yagni_complexity_critic) safety rationale -- that landing one ticket by hand through Claude Code interactively, with no new capability surface, is the lower-risk way to get a second real evidence data point precisely because fresh data-integrity bugs were just found -- is acknowledged as a position but dismissed mainly on 'it repeats the NSC-023 retrofit pattern' grounds, without weighing that the write-capability camp's alternative introduces new, unaudited risk surface at the same moment.","CURRENT_STATE.md's explicit statement that NSC-037 'is not the committed gameplay priority' is never reconciled with the synthesis naming NSC-037 as a next-slice candidate task."],"failure_if_followed":["The developer spends the remaining window building write capability, worktree isolation, a diff-scope checker, and a ported GER loop (a multi-week undertaking dressed as 'a slice'), discovers the unaddressed Unity Library/worktree cost problem mid-build, and stalls again with zero shipped gameplay -- reproducing the exact infrastructure-before-gameplay pattern the synthesis explicitly warns against.","Write capability ships scoped against exclusive_resources data that was patched via grep for one known bad string but not fully audited; a worker's first real write silently touches or corrupts a Unity scene/asset outside its intended scope because a different, still-undiscovered stale or ambiguous resource-path entry passed the new existence-only validator.","The team dispatches the newly-built write capability onto NSC-037 or NSC-024 for their unambiguity, producing a real conformant task and a satisfying pipeline proof point, while the actually-prioritized gameplay work (movement/input ownership questions blocking NSC-003) remains untouched, consuming scarce development time on the wrong feature.","Attempting the proposed 'lightweight section-anchor' GDD-drift check reveals the schema has no section-addressable structure to hang it on, turning a described one-line mitigation into another open-ended infrastructure sub-project."],"better_next_step_if_any":"Split the synthesis's next_slice into two genuinely separate, sequential slices. Slice A (safe, cheap, matches the manual/interactive minority's risk posture): do a full manual audit (not a single grep) of exclusive_resources across all 37 contracts and fix every stale/ambiguous entry; add the existence validator; give NSC-003 (or any task) a real schema-level way to represent \"blocked pending human design resolution\" instead of a one-off prose fix; then land one real ticket by having a human use Claude Code interactively -- no new AgentRuntime write capability -- to get a second genuine, pipeline-validated delivery-evidence record cheaply, choosing the task by actual game priority rather than pipeline-provability. Slice B (only after Slice A, and only after a written Unity Library/worktree-cache plan exists): add narrowly-scoped repository_write plus worktree isolation plus the ported GER loop as its own dedicated, separately-reviewed slice.","what_would_change_my_mind":["A full, non-grep audit confirming exclusive_resources integrity across all 37 task contracts with no further undiscovered stale/ambiguous entries.","A concrete, tested plan (even a short one) for Unity Library/ cache handling per Git worktree before recommending worktree-based agent write execution.","Independent verification that NSC-037 and/or NSC-024 have no open design questions and no graph/prose mismatch, plus an explicit reconciliation of NSC-037's 'not the committed gameplay priority' status with picking it as the delivery-lane task.","A rescoped next-slice description that fits in roughly a day or two of work rather than bundling write capability, isolation, a diff-scope checker, and a ported GER loop together.","A concrete demonstration that a lightweight, schema-supported GDD-section-drift check is actually buildable in short order given the current literal-text-quote representation, rather than requiring a GDD/schema restructuring."],"final_assessment":"synthesis_needs_revision","reason":"The synthesis correctly preserves the sound evidence/authority skeleton and correctly defers Milestone 2, dual-provider parity, and supervisor/worktree orchestration -- that part holds up under adversarial scrutiny and is well-grounded in verified repository evidence (confirmed independently: no diff/apply subcommands in taskcontrol.py, NSC-003's concrete/single_agent state contradicting CURRENT_STATE.md's documented open design questions, and the Reconciliation README's unfulfilled Milestone-1 promise). But its single concrete action item -- the recommended_next_slice -- does not hold up: it bundles write capability, worktree isolation, a diff-scope checker, a ported GER loop, and task re-verification into what it calls 'the minimum next increment,' scopes the new write capability's safety boundary against exclusive_resources data it just proved unreliable and admittedly never fully audited, and silently drops the one Unity-domain-specialist finding (Library/worktree cache cost) that bears directly on the isolation mechanism it recommends using. It also picks a next task partly for pipeline-provability over stated product priority without flagging that tradeoff. These are fixable with a materially smaller, resequenced slice (audit + fix + one manual interactive ticket first, write capability as a separate later slice) rather than a wholesale rejection of the synthesis's diagnosis -- hence revision, not unsafe."},"ttft_ms":5481,"ttft_stream_ms":4910,"time_to_request_ms":4244,"type":"result","duration_ms":209738,"uuid":"9cf58dfe-9e6e-4309-a2de-1528fa738a62"}
